Claims identified & fact-check:

Claim 1: Untreated ADHD has "bad consequences" and it's worth asking about the "side effects" of not treating it
→ Supported (general framing) — consistent with ICD-11/NICE guidance that untreated ADHD is associated with academic, occupational, and interpersonal impairment across the lifespan
Claim 2: Hallmark symptom: short attention span, "but not for everything"
→ Supported — matches ICD-11's note that inattention "may not be evident when the individual is engaged in activities that provide intense stimulation and frequent rewards"
Claim 3: Easily distracted — "the world comes at them too fast"
→ Supported — consistent with ICD-11 inattention criterion of being easily distracted by extraneous stimuli
Claim 4: Tend to be disorganized and procrastinate, not doing things "until someone's mad at them"
→ Supported — consistent with ICD-11 inattention criteria on difficulty planning, organizing, and completing tasks
Claim 5: Tend to have impulse control issues
→ Supported — impulsivity is a core symptom cluster in both DSM-5-TR and ICD-11
Claim 6: First step should be focusing on diet, with magnesium, zinc, and omega-3 fatty acids specifically named as helpful
→ Unsupported / contradicted — NICE guidance explicitly states there is no evidence to support dietary fatty acid supplementation for treating ADHD, and it does not recommend magnesium or zinc supplementation as a treatment either; NICE recommends assessing specific food/behavior links case-by-case rather than broadly promoting these supplements as a first-line intervention
Claim 7: If diet/supplements don't work, medication should then be used
→ Partially supported — NICE does support medication as an appropriate treatment when non-pharmacological approaches (specifically environmental modifications and, for adults, structured psychological interventions) have been tried and impairment persists; however, NICE does not frame dietary supplementation as the standard first-line non-pharmacological step — that role belongs to environmental modifications and parent-training/psychological interventions, so the sequencing described here is not accurate to the guideline
Claim 8: Untreated ADHD is associated with serious problems including addiction, school failure, and financial/occupational difficulties (e.g., "bankruptcy")
→ Supported (broadly) — ICD-11 and NICE both note associations between ADHD and substance misuse, academic underachievement, and occupational/financial difficulties (e.g., underemployment); "bankruptcy" specifically is a colorful extension but consistent with the documented occupational/financial impairment pattern

